course: medicine
lesson: 50
title: Heat, sun and cold on sports day
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your body is like an engine with a radiator: running makes heat, and sweat carries it away, but on a hot day you can lose more water than you take in and start to overheat.
explain: Olivia in Essex runs the 400 m on a 30°C sports day and ends up dizzy, sick, pale and sweaty with a headache: that is heat exhaustion, and shade, lying down with her feet up, sips of water and cooling her skin usually fix it within about 30 minutes.
explain: If someone is still unwell after 30 minutes of cooling, or is confused, hot but no longer sweating, has a fit or stops responding, it is heatstroke, and an adult calls 999.
explain: Cold is a danger too: on a dreich, Baltic morning in Edinburgh (Scots for grey and drizzly, and slang for freezing), cold muscles strain more easily, and a child who is shivering hard, pale and mumbling must be warmed slowly, never dumped in a hot bath.
fact: Heat exhaustion signs include headache, dizziness, feeling sick, being very thirsty, heavy sweating, pale clammy skin and cramps, and children can get it as well as adults.
fact: For heat exhaustion: move to a cool or shady place, lie down with the feet raised a little, sip water, cool the skin, and tell an adult; it should get better within about 30 minutes.
fact: Heatstroke is more serious, and heat exhaustion that is ignored can turn into it: still unwell after 30 minutes of cooling, confused, hot but not sweating, a fit or not responding means an adult calls 999.
fact: Water is the best drink for children during sport; energy drinks are full of caffeine and sugar and are not for children.
fact: Pouring water over your head cools the skin a little, but only drinking puts back the water lost in sweat.
fact: UV light from the sun gets through cloud, so sun cream is still needed on grey days.
fact: Sun cream should be put on again at least every 2 hours and after sweating hard or towelling, because sweat and towels wipe it off.
fact: The sun's UV is strongest around the middle of the day, roughly 11 am to 3 pm in the UK summer, so shade between events and a hat help.
fact: In great heat adults can pause or move events, and a friend who looks wobbly should be reported to an adult at once.
fact: Cold muscles are stiffer and easier to strain, so warm layers and a proper warm-up help on cold days.
fact: Shivering hard, pale skin, slurred speech and being very sleepy can be signs a person is getting dangerously cold.
fact: Warm a cold person slowly: indoors, dry clothes, blankets and a warm drink; the NHS says not to use a hot bath or rub their arms and legs, and very drowsy or not responding means 999.
fact: You still lose water in the cold through sweat and breath, and you may feel less thirsty, so you still need to drink.
fact: «Togs» is Australian for swimming costume, «stinker» means a very hot day there, and in Canada a «toque» is a woolly hat.
exercise: What does sweat do? | It carries heat away from the body | It makes muscles stronger | It cleans the blood of germs and dirt | It stops you getting thirsty
exercise: Which is a sign of heat exhaustion? | Dizziness and feeling sick in the heat | A little sunburn on the nose | Feeling hungry after a race | Muddy trainers and wet socks after a race
exercise: Can children get heat exhaustion? | Yes, children can get it too | No, only grown-ups ever get it | Only babies and toddlers get it | Only children over twelve get it
exercise: What helps heat exhaustion? | Shade, lie down, feet up, sips of water | Run another race to sweat it out | Sit in the sun to dry off | A big fizzy drink in one go
exercise: How soon should someone with heat exhaustion improve with cooling? | Within about 30 minutes | Within about 30 seconds | After about 3 days | They never improve without hospital
exercise: Which means heatstroke and a 999 call? | Confused and hot but not sweating | Thirsty after a race | Red cheeks after running | Tired and yawning at the end of the day
exercise: Best drink for kids during sport? | Water | An energy drink | A cola | A coffee
exercise: Does pouring water over your head replace the water you sweat out? | No, only drinking does that | Yes, the skin soaks it all up | Yes, if the water is cold | Only if you pour two bottles
exercise: Do you need sun cream on a cloudy day? | Yes, UV gets through cloud | No, the clouds block all the UV | Only if it's hotter than 30°C | Only on your face and your nose
exercise: How often should you put sun cream on again? | At least every 2 hours and after sweating | Once in the morning is enough | Only when you already feel your skin burning | Every 10 minutes exactly
exercise: When is the sun's UV strongest? | Around the middle of the day | First thing in the morning, at dawn | Just before the sun goes down | At exactly the same level all day
exercise: Why wear warm layers and warm up on a freezing morning? | Cold muscles are stiffer and strain more easily | Cold makes bones melt | Warm clothes make you faster | The ref says so
exercise: How should you warm up someone who is too cold? | Slowly, indoors, dry clothes and blankets | Straight into a hot bath | Rub their arms and legs hard | Make them sprint round the pitch
exercise: Do you need to drink in cold weather? | Yes, you still lose water | No, cold means no water needed | Only if you sweat a lot | Only hot chocolate counts
exercise: What's a toque in Canada? | A woolly hat | A water bottle | A hockey stick | A pair of gloves
test: Sam's been in the shade 30 minutes, still confused, hot but not sweating. Teacher: «he's just sleepy». What now? | An adult calls 999, this could be heatstroke | Let him sleep it off in the tent | Give him an energy drink | Send him back to run the relay
test: Jordan in Texas: «water's mid, this energy drink hits different, no cap». Better choice? | Water, sipped little and often | The energy drink, it's made for sport | Both, mixed together | Nothing, drinking slows you
test: Aidan: «don't need sun cream, it's dead grey». Four races later? | He can still burn; UV gets through cloud | He's safe, grey days have no sun at all, mate | He's safe if he wears a vest | He only burns if it rains
test: Lachlan in Brisbane, sports day 11 till 3, «stinker of a day». Smartest plan between events? | Wait in the shade with a hat and water | Sunbathe to keep muscles warm | Wear a black top to keep cool in the sun | Skip drinks so you don't need the loo
test: Amelia pours water on her head: «Sorted, don't need to drink now». Right? | No, she still needs to drink | Yes, her skin soaks it up | Yes, cold water counts double | No, she needs to pour more
test: Mr Okafor stops the races for ten minutes in the shade. A kid moans «that's well tight». Was he right? | Yes, safety in the heat comes first | No, sports day must go on no matter what | No, only rain can stop races | Yes, but only for the teachers
test: Rory, 3°C in Edinburgh: «base layers are for the nesh». What would happen if he skips them and the warm-up? | His cold muscles would strain more easily | He'd play better from the cold | Nothing, cold doesn't affect muscles at all | He'd get sunburnt
test: Callie's shivering hard and mumbling after a wet match. Her dad wants a hot bath. Better? | Warm her slowly indoors with dry clothes and blankets | Hot bath straight away | Rub her arms and legs hard | Send her for a run to warm up
test: Noah in Ottawa: «it's cold, I don't need water, eh». True? | No, you still lose water in the cold | Yes, cold weather needs no drinks | Yes, as long as he wears his toque, eh | No, he needs an energy drink
test: Olivia's pale, sweaty, dizzy after the 400 m on a 30°C day. Her mate says «only grown-ups get heat exhaustion». So? | Children get it too; shade, feet up, sips of water | Her mate is right, she's fine | She should run it off in the sun | She should drink a cola fast
test: What would happen if a hot, wobbly runner ignored the signs and ran three more races? | Heat exhaustion could turn into heatstroke | She'd sweat the illness out and feel better | She'd cool down by running | Nothing, races cool you
exam: Identify the course of action consistent with established guidance in respect of a child exhibiting signs of heat exhaustion. | Removal to a cool place, lying with the feet raised, and the taking of water | Continued participation in order to induce perspiration | Exposure to direct sunlight in order to dry the skin | The consumption of a caffeinated beverage
exam: It is given that a child remains confused and is no longer perspiring notwithstanding thirty minutes of cooling. State the appropriate action. | Emergency services are to be summoned | The child is to be permitted to sleep | The child is to resume participation | The child is to be given an energy drink
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning ultraviolet radiation on an overcast day. | Ultraviolet radiation penetrates cloud cover | Cloud cover eliminates ultraviolet radiation entirely | Ultraviolet radiation is present only above thirty degrees | Ultraviolet radiation is present only at dawn
exam: Ascertain the method recommended for the rewarming of a child exhibiting signs of excessive cold. | Gradual rewarming indoors with dry clothing and blankets | Immediate immersion in a hot bath | Vigorous rubbing of the arms and legs | Strenuous running in the open air
